<Page path="/compress-image-to-1mb/">
  <h1>Compress an image to under 1 MB</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    One megabyte is generous. For most photos it is not a squeeze at all: the picture keeps its size and loses very little. The
    interesting questions are which format to use and what to do about the occasional huge original.
  </p>
  <p><a class="btn primary" href="/?limit=1mb">Open the tool set to 1 MB</a></p>

  <h2>What we measured</h2>
  <p>
    The same ten phone photos as in the <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb/">100 KB guide</a> (eight at 3 megapixels, two at 12),
    with a 1 MB limit on Quick effort:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Every photo kept its full size.</strong> None had to shrink.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The 3-megapixel photos came out at 0.46 to 0.83 MB</strong>, below the limit, because they had reached the top of the
      quality scale (100) and there was nothing more to spend the budget on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The 12-megapixel photos used the whole megabyte</strong> (0.93 and 1.0 MB) at JPEG quality 76 and 77, which is
      still a very good-looking 12-megapixel picture.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="small">Ten photos from one phone, one run each, desktop engine. Results vary with how much fine detail a picture holds.</p>

  <h2>Which format</h2>
  <p>
    JPEG won almost every time at this size, with WebP ahead on one photo. That is typical: at generous sizes the formats'
    advantages shrink, and JPEG opens everywhere. If you plan to put the file somewhere that does not accept WebP or AVIF, force
    JPEG in the Format box and you lose almost nothing at 1 MB. Under tighter limits the choice matters more; see
    <a href="/jpeg-webp-avif-png/">the format guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What to watch for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Email.</strong> Attachments are usually encoded for email, which adds about a third to their size, and limits
      apply to the whole message. A 1 MB photo is fine; five of them may not be. See
      <a href="/common-size-limits/">common limits</a> for Gmail's rule and where it came from.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Screenshots.</strong> They are usually already well under 1 MB as PNG. KVGrainy will keep them lossless when it can.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Huge scans or panoramas.</strong> A 50-megapixel image does not fit under 1 MB at full size however clever the
      search is; it will shrink. The page refuses inputs above 50 megapixels in the browser (the desktop app has no such limit).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why not just lower the quality slider?</h2>
  <p>
    Because quality and size interact differently for every picture. A single slider cannot tell that this photo is better off
    smaller, or that this one should be WebP. KVGrainy searches both, so you choose the outcome (a size) and it finds the settings.
    That is the whole idea; <a href="/how-it-works/">how it works</a> has the details.
  </p>
</Page>
